<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Epidaurus</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Epidaurus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Argolid city-state whose sanctuary of Asclepius became the most famous healing centre of the Greek world, with its magnificent theatre.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Epidaurus lay on a small peninsula on the east coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> Dorian settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/argos">Argos</a> made it an independent city; tradition held that Epidaurians in turn settled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aegina">Aegina</a>. Its sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> Maleatas inland gradually gave way to the cult of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asclepius">Asclepius</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Epidaurus was governed by an oligarchy of 180 citizens. From the late 5th century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctuary-of-asclepius-at-epidaurus">Asclepieion</a> attracted sick pilgrims who slept in the sanctuary hoping for healing dreams, and inscriptions record their cures. The 4th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polykleitos-the-younger">Polykleitos the Younger</a>, seats about 13,000. Epidaurus was a loyal member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Epidaurus joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League</a> in 243 BCE and passed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 146 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> plundered the sanctuary in 87 BCE, but it recovered and flourished in Roman times; its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> still hosts performances.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/epidaurus.jpg" alt="Epidaurus" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Jean Housen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20190511_172_epidaure.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Epidaurus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Asclepius</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Epidauros</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-523&amp;focus=epidaurus" aria-label="Epidaurus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5258 824 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Epidaurus lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5578 1080L5562 1098M5578 1080L5549 1060"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5562 1098h0M5549 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5578 1080h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5578 1080h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-523&amp;focus=epidaurus">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

